<Guide slug="typhoon-season" photo="okinawa">
  <p>
    Typhoons are the Western Pacific’s hurricanes. They can form in any month, but most develop between <strong>June and
    November</strong>, with the peak in <strong>August and September</strong>. An average year brings around 25 named storms
    to the region, of which a handful affect Japan, Taiwan, the Philippines, Hong Kong, southern China or Vietnam.
  </p>
  <p>
    Unlike a rainy season, a typhoon usually disrupts travel for one to three days: flights and ferries are cancelled,
    trains stop and shops close while it passes. The risk on any given week is low — but it’s worth booking flexibly if you
    travel in late summer.
  </p>

  <h2>Typhoon risk by destination</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Japan:</b> August–September for the main islands; July–September for <a href="/destinations/okinawa/">Okinawa</a>, which sits on the main typhoon track. <a href="/destinations/hokkaido/">Hokkaido</a> is rarely hit.</li>
    <li><b>Taiwan:</b> July–September; typhoons often bring heavy rain to the mountains and east coast.</li>
    <li><b>Hong Kong and southern China:</b> June–October. Hong Kong’s warning system matters: when Signal No. 8 is raised, public transport winds down and most businesses close.</li>
    <li><b>The Philippines:</b> June–November, peaking August–October, mainly in Luzon and the Visayas. Mindanao and southern Palawan are rarely hit.</li>
    <li><b>Vietnam:</b> August–November; the central coast (Da Nang, Hoi An) has its typhoon and flood season in October–November.</li>
    <li><b>South Korea and Shanghai:</b> occasional storms in August and September.</li>
  </ul>

