  <h2>Where to go instead in August and September</h2>
  <p>
    Typhoons don’t form near the equator, so the dry season in Indonesia is a natural alternative: <a href="/destinations/bali/">Bali</a>,
    <a href="/destinations/lombok/">Lombok</a> and <a href="/destinations/komodo/">Komodo</a> are at their best. Malaysian
    <a href="/destinations/borneo/">Borneo</a> and <a href="/destinations/singapore/">Singapore</a> are also outside the typhoon belt,
    and northern Japan (Hokkaido) has its pleasant summer.
  </p>

  <h2>Travelling in typhoon season</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Buy travel insurance when you book,</b> before a storm is named, and check it covers weather disruption.</li>
    <li><b>Keep a buffer day</b> before international flights home.</li>
    <li><b>Follow official warnings:</b> the Japan Meteorological Agency, the Hong Kong Observatory, Taiwan’s Central Weather Administration and PAGASA in the Philippines.</li>
    <li><b>Prefer refundable bookings</b> for island hops and ferries, which are the first to be cancelled.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>See also: <a href="/guides/monsoon-season-asia/">monsoon season in Asia</a> and <a href="/guides/japan-rainy-season/">Japan’s rainy season</a>.</p>
</Guide>
